When you add a new template you only need to keep the JSON shape in sync with the placeholders. The front‑end can stay generic (all sections are optional); the server will automatically “skip” any empty array because Mustache only renders sections that have truthy data.

5️⃣ PDF Generation – No Code Change Needed
Your existing /api/cv/download/:cvId route already:

Fetches the template’s html_template and stylesheet.
Calls Mustache.render(template, { ...data, color1: '#667eea', color2: '#764ba2' }).
Starts a headless Chromium (Puppeteer) and prints the rendered page to PDF.
Since the new template follows the same contract ({{color1}}, {{color2}}), the same endpoint will perfectly generate the PDF for any of the 50+ templates—including the Prateek one.

6️⃣ Preview the Template in the UI
Add a preview image (public/templates/prateek/preview.png).
The TemplatePicker component you already have will automatically show that image because the API returns the preview_img column.
When the user selects this template, the colour picker works as before, and the live preview (your existing <iframe> that renders the Mustache output) will instantly show the real‑look resume.
If you want a live preview while editing, just feed the current formData JSON to the back‑end via a tiny endpoint (POST /api/cv/render-preview) that returns the rendered HTML (no PDF). The front‑end can embed it in an <iframe> without a round‑trip to PDF. This is optional; the existing client‑side Mustache rendering you already have works fine.

7️⃣ Full‑Stack Security Recap (Why This Remains Safe)
Layer	What’s Applied	How it protects the new template
Transport	TLS termination at Nginx, HSTS header	Prevents MITM on the PDF/download request.
Auth	JWT access token (15 min) + refresh token (7 days) stored in HttpOnly, SameSite‑Strict cookies	Only a verified user can call /api/cv/download.
Rate‑Limiting	express-rate-limit globally + per‑endpoint for /login, /signup, /download	Stops brute‑force and mass PDF download attacks.
Input Sanitisation	sanitize-html runs on the JSON payload before saving, plus server‑side schema validation (express-validator)	Blocks XSS even if a user tries to inject <script> into a free‑form field.
SQL Injection	All queries are parameterised ($1, $2…).	No string interpolation → safe DB access.
File Upload	Multer validation (image/* MIME, 2 MiB limit, random UUID filename, stored outside web root).	Avatar upload can’t become a weapon.
CSRF	SameSite‑Strict cookies + JWT verification.	No cross‑origin state‑changing requests.
Audit Logging	login_logs, signup_logs, download can be added similarly.	Every PDF download can be tracked if you decide to add a download_logs table.
CSP	Helmet inserts Content‑Security‑Policy that disallows inline scripts, frames only from self.	Even if a malicious user managed to store <script> it will never run.
8️⃣ How to Add More Templates in the Future
Create HTML (Mustache) & CSS files.
Put a 200 × 200 px thumbnail in your static folder.
Run an INSERT like in step 3, using your new thumbnail URL.
Optionally update the global “template count” cache if you have one (the front‑end simply reads the API list).
Tip: If you have dozens of templates, keep them under templates/ and add a tiny seed script (npm run seed:templates) that loops over a folder, reads each template.html & style.css, and executes the INSERT for you. This makes onboarding new designs a one‑liner.

